{"timestamp_edt": "2026-08-19 15:50:37 EDT", "action_type": "query", "duration_seconds": 13.452851, "file_name": "", "query_text": "What are you saying?", "steps_taken": [{"step": "embed query", "seconds": 2.690385}, {"step": "keyword extraction", "seconds": 0.000422}, {"step": "vector retrieval", "seconds": 0.246002}, {"step": "context assembly", "seconds": 1e-06}, {"step": "llm answer generation", "seconds": 10.514175}], "tools_called": ["RetrievalService.search_similar_chunks", "RetrievalService.search_ancestry_data"], "api_calls_made": ["embedding (ollama)", "llm (deepseek)"], "final_response": "The documents are saying the following:\n\n- **Edward Gowen 3rd** was classified as “black” under tax law, which was a legal marker used to establish racial identity and to require taxation on himself, his spouse, and daughters. This classification differed from being called “mulatto” in military records, which had little meaning beyond identification. [Document 1]\n- Virginia’s 1643 and North Carolina’s 1749 tax laws imposed taxes on Black and mulatto women over ages sixteen and twelve respectively, while white women were absolutely not taxed. These laws provide evidence of racial classification. [Document 1]\n- Edward Gowen 3rd lived in the same physical location while county boundary lines shifted around him, which is why he appears in records of different counties. He was originally taxable in predecessor Edgecombe County, where he refused to pay taxes on his tithables. [Document 2]\n- In August 1756, Edgecombe County’s Attorney General prosecuted Edward Gowen 3rd for concealing his tithables. The 1765 Tax List shows he still refused to list his wife as tithable in the part of Edgecombe County that later became Granville County. This prosecution was in response to the new 1749 North Carolina law modeled after Virginia. [Document 2]\n- Black men in North Carolina were required by law to pay taxes on their wives, unlike white men. Many refused as a matter of principle and would declare their wives and daughters were not Black to avoid the additional taxes. [footnote 563]\n- A separate case concerns **Benjamin D Brooker** of Hingham, Plymouth County, Massachusetts. He traveled about thirty miles to the Norfolk Court of Common Pleas to apply in person. His annual income as a laborer was $100, he owned no real estate, and his wife was listed as an invalid. [Document 3]\n- In 1822, Brooker’s estate was appraised at $34.58, but estate fees and travel expenses totaled $34.50, leaving only eight cents. Administrator Samuel Sprague wrote that the estate was insufficient to pay Brooker’s bills, and the probate judge wrote that “there is nothing remaining to distribute.” No pension records are mentioned for him in the provided excerpt. [Document 3]", "response_time_seconds": 10.514175, "input_tokens": 1472, "output_tokens": 648, "input_cache_hit_tokens": 128, "input_cache_miss_tokens": 1344, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.002173, "pricing_rate": "off-peak", "reasoning_mode": "low"}
